and Rust\nreturn the SAME error code (not merely \"both failed\"). Highlights you must get right:\n- `LZ4F_getBlockSize` with blockSizeID in {1,2,3}, > 7, and NEGATIVE (out-of-range enum values\n  passed across FFI — C enums accept any int) => `lz4f_err(2)`; 0 => 65536; {4,5,6,7} => valid sizes.\n- `LZ4F_isError` for {0,1,23,24,25, (usize)-1, -23, -24, -25}; `LZ4F_getErrorName` for a non-error\n  code => \"Unspecified error code\", and for every ordinal 1..=23 => the matching string (compare\n  the C strings byte-for-byte via `CStr`); `LZ4F_getErrorCode`.\n- `LZ4F_createCompressionContext(NULL, ...)` => `lz4f_err(21)`; version != 100 => 0 (NOT validated).\n- `LZ4F_freeCompressionContext(NULL)` / `LZ4F_freeDecompressionContext(NULL)` => 0.\n- `LZ4F_createDecompressionContext(NULL, ...)` => `lz4f_err(21)`; version != 100 => 0.\n- `LZ4F_freeDecompressionContext` on a MID-FRAME dctx returns the internal `dStage` number (a small\n  positive value, NOT an error) — assert C and Rust return the same number.\n- `LZ4F_compressBegin` with dstCapacity 0..18 => `lz4f_err(11)`; exactly 19 => success.\n- `LZ4F_compressBegin` with prefs.blockSizeID in {1,2,3,8} is NOT rejected — it SUCCEEDS and\n  `maxBlockSize` silently becomes `(size_t)-2`. Assert both return the same success value.\n- `LZ4F_compressBegin_usingDict` with `dictSize > INT_MAX` => `lz4f_err(4)`. (Pass a huge size with\n  a real small buffer — the check happens before any read.)\n- `LZ4F_compressUpdate` / `LZ4F_uncompressedUpdate` on a cctx that was never `compressBegin`'d, and\n  again after a successful `compressEnd` => `lz4f_err(20)`.\n- `LZ4F_compressUpdate` with dstCapacity one below `LZ4F_compressBound(...)` => `lz4f_err(11)`;\n  exactly at the bound => success. `LZ4F_uncompressedUpdate` with dstCapacity < srcSize => `lz4f_err(11)`.\n- `LZ4F_flush` on an uninitialized cctx (tmpInSize == 0) => 0, NOT an error. With buffered data and\n  cStage != 1 => `lz4f_err(20)`. dstCapacity < tmpInSize+8 => `lz4f_err(11)`.\n